Quoting  Erik  Piper <erik@sky.cz>:
>Jove wrote:
>>So it doesn't seem so much that players think Nethack is too
>>easy.  It's more that they don't think Nethack is interesting
>>enough.

I don't think this is really true. I think the "why make the game harder,
there's always conducts" argument is fundamentally flawed; you might as
well ask why the game kills you when you run out of hitpoints. Why not
just have a "quit at negative HP" conduct?

I don't really want to make a game that doesn't exploit endless grind or
some of the non-grind cheeses any harder. That's a solid challenging game.
What I want is to eliminate the utility of grinding and other cheeses.

Part of this is I think the game should be challenging in and of itself,
without binding yourself in a tissue of artificial restrictions. The parts
of the game where you simply can play all-out - the opening game - are the
most interesting.

>It doesn't seem to me -- again as a casual observer -- that that's
>really an option for Nethack, as fundamentally changing the nature of
>the food clock would just *not* be cool; it just wouldn't be Nethack
>anymore, right?

Certainly the food clock cannot practically be extended into the middle
game. I'd still like a middle game clock of some kind - not a rigid one,
but one that increases difficulty if you mess around. (Of course, food is
like this to a degree; yes, starving kills you, but you can consume
prayer, another limited resource, making other things more difficult.)

Mind you, I don't have a big problem with Gehennom, not that I don't think
it could improve. It's easy to arrange to explore it quickly (and that to
a degree is a problem in efficent resource usage) and on the way back up I
am worried and nervous which always increases interest.
